  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian Pacific Consortium of Veterinary Epidemiologists (APCOVE) project conducts online and face-to-face veterinary epidemiology training programs to participants in Vietnam, Philippines, Malaysia and Indonesia. Fellows are all highly trained professionals in their field, and the training enables them to extend their skills and knowledge to protect their countries from outbreaks. One graduate from the Philippines is the City Veterinarian of Bais City, who conducted a study assessing the biosecurity practices of swine farms as part of her training. The study identified key gaps in biosecurity practices and when African Swine Fever (ASF) reached Bais city in July 2023, her study was instrumental in shaping local policy, and prioritising outbreak response activities. The graduate was also designated as the Incident Commander during the outbreak, which allowed her to apply and share the practical elements of the coursework, including leading the team to undertake a rapid risk assessment, surveillance and monitoring, strengthening of biosecurity measures, and awareness campaigns. Thanks to the coordinated efforts of various departments and agencies, ASF was successfully contained in Bais City within 6 weeks.</narrative>
    </description>
